## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design forward- and backward-compatible APIs—a common real-world constraint during model upgrades.

## Key Concepts
- Version negotiation and routing
- Compatibility shim and input mapping
- Migration plan, deprecation notices, and rollback strategy
- Canary rollout and feature flags
- Observability: parity checks and rollback triggers

## Implementation Approach
Implement version-aware routing in /cca_score/compute. Maintain a lightweight compatibility shim that maps v1 inputs to v2 scoring logic, ensuring output parity where possible. Include migration_status fields to track client version transitions, and add deprecation_notice in responses for outdated clients. Use model_version headers or query parameters for routing decisions, with graceful defaults when clients omit version information. The config endpoint (POST /cca_score/config) returns the latest supported version and feature flags, enabling clients to self-upgrade. Store client_timestamp to enable time-based analysis of migration progress.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo mapping: v1 -> v2 input schema
function mapV1toV2(v1) {
  return {
    userId: v1.user_id,
    locale: v1.locale,
    region: v1.region,
    modelVersion: 'v2',
    clientTs: v1.client_timestamp
  };
}

// Version-aware routing
function routeRequest(req) {
  const version = req.headers['x-model-version'] || 'v1';
  if (version === 'v1') return handleV1(mapV1toV2(req.body));
  return handleV2(req.body);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This scenario tests real-time, multi-tenant cca_score with strict latency and verifiable provenance, requiring concrete API design, data modeling, and ledger trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time ingestion, per-tenant isolation, tamper-evident ledger (append-only log or Merkle root)
- Minimal data model with nonce and signature for replay protection
- Drift/poisoning detection, regional latency considerations
- Blockchain vs centralized ledger trade-offs (cost, latency, auditability)

## Code Example
```javascript
type ScoreEvent = {
  tenant_id: string;
  device_id_masked: string;
  timestamp: number;
  score_share: number;
  nonce: string;
  signature: string;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prove ledger integrity to auditors without exposing raw data?
- How would you handle cross-region residency and regulatory constraints (GDPR/CCPA) in this design?

